Ah yep, good catch – “system message” is a bit abstract if you’re just in the normal ChatGPT app.

Here’s what it means **and where to put it**, depending on how you’re using this DJ agent:

---

## 1. In the normal ChatGPT app (no custom GPTs)

You don’t really *have* a visible “system message” box here.

**What to do instead:**

1. Start a **brand new chat**.
2. Paste the whole DJ-agent block in as your **very first message**.
3. After that, talk to it normally (e.g. “2-hour set, 11pm–1am warehouse, 126–130 BPM, help me build crates”).

In this mode, that long prompt is just acting *like* a system message because it sets the rules before anything else.

---

## 2. In a **Custom GPT** (the “Create a GPT” screen in ChatGPT)

This is the closest to a real “system message” for you.

1. Click your name / profile → **“My GPTs”** → **“Create a GPT”**.
2. Go to the **Configure** tab.
3. In the big **“Instructions”** / “What should this GPT do?” area, paste the DJ-agent prompt.
4. Give it a name like `FØPA – DJ Selector Co-Pilot`.
5. Save it.

Now you’ve literally turned that into your own DJ agent. Any time you select that GPT, it will behave with those rules.

---

## 3. If you’re using the **OpenAI API / code**

Then “system message” is literal:

```jsonc
{
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "system",
      "content": "<PASTE THE DJ-AGENT PROMPT HERE>"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I’ve got a 2-hour set at 126–128 BPM, help structure crates."
    }
  ]
}
```

